{"kind":"assert","text":"Decision on `7182e52e`, the two C4 prerequisites.\n\nThe Explore bridge\n\n1. C4 owns the bridge. Its submit is the first caller, by item 3 of\n   amendment `c0567f5a`. The Replica owns the operation: it gives\n   `actExplore` the real authority, owners and evidence, and it\n   answers `unavailable` when it holds none. The package entry does\n   not export it. No raw state leaves the Replica, and no second\n   evaluator exists.\n2. C4 also owns what items 1 to 4 of the amendment name: the `ref`\n   of the row that a draft opened on, the changed fields, the\n   returned `now`, and the new version after a return.\n3. E3 keeps the rest: Explore again at each view and each tick,\n   `offers`, a `ref` with its sources, `recorded`, and one identity\n   for a row. E3 calls the same Replica operation. Start the bridge\n   now. It does not wait for E3.\n4. The draft-ownership check runs inside the transaction of E4's\n   submit, under the one fence, before the permission mark. Give\n   that transaction one internal precondition that the draft layer\n   supplies. A failed precondition stops `taken` and writes nothing.\n   No second fence, and no public entry that skips the fence.\n\nThe browser authorization store\n\n5. A separate request holds it, and I file it now as E4-B. C4 needs\n   it and does not own it. It has its own promise, report and\n   review scope. Say whether it lands in the C4 head or in a head\n   before it.\n6. The removal rule: the authorization history of a key lives as\n   long as the custody record of that key, live or archived. It\n   leaves the store in the same transaction that removes that\n   record, and at no other time. If the custody archive removes\n   nothing today, the authorization store removes nothing, and the\n   note says so.\n7. The bound of 64 positions for each key stands.\n\nThe draft port with its own owner, scope, batch read, write and\nsignals, on the existing coordinating fence, is accepted as you\ndescribe it.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}